  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The models were developed and validated using historical GIDAS datasets, and their real-world operational utility is still undergoing prospective multicenter validation.",
      "what": "This study developed and validated logistic regression models to predict specific, clinically relevant injury patterns for front-row vehicle occupants using German In-Depth Accident Study (GIDAS) data. The models utilize crash severity (Energy Equivalent Speed), restraint use, and vehicle characteristics as predictors, achieving high discrimination with an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AROC) above 0.85 for most injury groups. External validation on a recent GIDAS subset confirmed the reproducibility of predictions for head, thorax, and overall severe injuries.",
      "why": "It establishes a manufacturer-independent framework that can translate raw crash telemetry into immediate, region-specific injury predictions. This capability allows next-generation eCall systems to provide emergency dispatchers with precise triage intelligence for allocating specialized trauma resources before responders arrive."
    }
  },
